{"id": "7501934752c37354", "timestamp_utc": "2026-08-28T11:58:36.205846+00:00", "author": "high2", "kind": "proof_attempt", "claim": "Subgoal solved: every word with m-1 copies of D and n-1 copies of L is obtainable from D^(m-1)L^(n-1) by DL -> LD, so there are binomial(m+n-2,m-1) reachable configurations.", "evidence": "Use induction on word length. If a target word begins with D, keep the first D fixed and apply induction to the remaining D^(m-2)L^(n-1). If it begins with L, successively swap the first L in D^(m-1)L^(n-1) left across all m-1 D letters, giving L D^(m-1)L^(n-2), and apply induction to the suffix. Boundary cases with only one letter type are immediate. Each word determines a unique cell-path, and hence a unique complementary coin configuration. The number of such words is the number of choices of the m-1 positions occupied by D, namely binomial(m+n-2,m-1).", "verifiable": true, "status": "unverified", "fact_id": null, "links": {"plan_id": "hole_path_flip", "attempt_type": "direct", "subgoal": "Prove all monotone path words are reachable and enumerate them.", "attempt_summary": "A leading-letter induction reaches every shuffle word; ordinary word counting yields the binomial coefficient.", "status": "solved", "used_examples": [], "used_counterexamples": [], "counterexample_search_for_stuck_subgoal": {"performed": false, "summary": "The subgoal was proved directly.", "result": "not_needed"}, "key_stuck_points": [], "used_results": [], "adapted_from": [], "migration_failures": [], "branch_id": "hole_path_flip"}, "glossary": {"D": "a one-square downward step in the hole path", "L": "a one-square leftward step in the hole path"}}
{"id": "f562ac0d49300017", "timestamp_utc": "2026-08-28T11:59:20.142527+00:00", "author": "high3", "kind": "proof_attempt", "claim": "The reachable coin configurations are in bijection with monotone lattice paths from (1,n) to (m,1), hence their number is \\(\\binom{m+n-2}{m-1}\\).", "evidence": "Orient the grid so that increasing the first coordinate is an eastward step E and decreasing the second coordinate is a southward step S. Initially the unoccupied squares are exactly the top row together with the rightmost column, a path from (1,n) to (m,1) with word E^(m-1)S^(n-1). Suppose a legal move slides the coin at (i,j) to (i+1,j+1). Just before the move the three empty squares (i,j+1),(i+1,j+1),(i+1,j) form an ES corner of the current empty path; afterward the empty squares (i,j+1),(i,j),(i+1,j) form the corresponding SE corner. Thus induction shows that every reachable configuration has as its empty set such a path. Conversely, take any such path and place coins on its complement. If its word is not E^(m-1)S^(n-1), it has an adjacent SE. The three squares of that corner are empty, while the northeast fourth square is not on the monotone path and therefore contains a coin. Sliding that coin northwest is the reverse of a legal move and changes SE to ES. This reduces by one the number of pairs in which an S precedes an E, so repetition reaches the initial path. Reversing these slides reaches the chosen path by legal moves. Distinct paths have distinct empty sets, and choosing the locations of the m-1 E steps among m+n-2 steps gives the stated binomial coefficient.", "verifiable": true, "status": "unverified", "fact_id": null, "links": {"source": "2023 Putnam B1 empty-path bijection"}, "glossary": {"E": "an eastward unit step, increasing the first grid coordinate by 1", "S": "a southward unit step, decreasing the second grid coordinate by 1"}}
{"id": "cb6b60de3da445a8", "timestamp_utc": "2026-08-28T11:59:25.756504+00:00", "author": "xhigh4", "kind": "proof_attempt", "claim": "Subgoal solved: every configuration reachable from the initial one has as its unoccupied squares exactly the vertices of a monotone path from (1,n) to (m,1), and one legal move changes an adjacent ES in the path word to SE.", "evidence": "Take E to increase the first coordinate by 1 and S to decrease the second coordinate by 1. Initially the unoccupied squares are the top row together with the rightmost column, exactly the vertices of E^(m-1)S^(n-1). Inductively suppose the holes are a path. A legal move from (i,j) to (i+1,j+1) is allowed precisely when (i,j+1), (i+1,j+1), and (i+1,j) are holes. In the monotone path these are consecutive vertices joined by E then S. The move fills (i+1,j+1) and empties (i,j), leaving (i,j+1),(i,j),(i+1,j), which are consecutive vertices joined by S then E. All other holes are unchanged, so the new holes are the vertex set of the path obtained by ES -> SE.", "verifiable": true, "status": "unverified", "fact_id": null, "links": {"plan_id": "hole-path-bijection", "attempt_type": "direct", "status": "solved", "subgoal": "path invariance", "counterexample_search_for_stuck_subgoal": {"performed": false, "result": "not_needed"}, "used_results": [], "adapted_from": ["Official 2023 Putnam B1 Solution 1 path-flip idea"], "key_stuck_points": []}, "glossary": {"E": "A unit east step, increasing the first coordinate i by 1.", "S": "A unit south step, decreasing the second coordinate j by 1."}}
{"id": "d53888264eb65ebc", "timestamp_utc": "2026-08-28T11:59:25.787248+00:00", "author": "xhigh4", "kind": "proof_attempt", "claim": "Subgoal solved: every monotone path from (1,n) to (m,1) is the hole set of a reachable configuration.", "evidence": "Represent the path by a word with m-1 letters E and n-1 letters S. If the word is not E^(m-1)S^(n-1), then some S occurs before some E; at the boundary between a nonempty block ending in S and the following block beginning in E there is an adjacent SE. Its three path vertices are (i,j+1),(i,j),(i+1,j), so they are holes. The fourth square (i+1,j+1) is not a path vertex and therefore contains a coin because the configuration places coins on every non-path square. Slide this coin from (i+1,j+1) to (i,j). This is the reverse of a legal move: in the resulting configuration the source (i,j) has a coin and the other three squares of the 2-by-2 block are empty. At word level the reverse slide changes SE to ES. Define the inversion number to be the number of ordered pairs in which an S occurs before an E. Each SE -> ES lowers it by exactly one. Repetition therefore terminates at inversion number zero, whose unique word is E^(m-1)S^(n-1), the initial path. Reversing all slides gives legal moves from the initial configuration to the prescribed path configuration.", "verifiable": true, "status": "unverified", "fact_id": null, "links": {"plan_id": "hole-path-bijection", "attempt_type": "direct", "status": "solved", "subgoal": "surjectivity of path encoding", "counterexample_search_for_stuck_subgoal": {"performed": false, "result": "not_needed"}, "used_results": [], "adapted_from": ["Official 2023 Putnam B1 Solution 1 reverse-corner idea"], "key_stuck_points": []}, "glossary": {"E": "A unit east step, increasing the first coordinate i by 1.", "S": "A unit south step, decreasing the second coordinate j by 1.", "inversion number": "The number of pairs of positions in a path word at which an S precedes an E."}}
{"id": "6d58658a315825c5", "timestamp_utc": "2026-08-28T11:59:25.819526+00:00", "author": "xhigh4", "kind": "proof_attempt", "claim": "Subgoal solved: the monotone-path configurations are counted by binomial(m+n-2,m-1), including the degenerate cases m=1 or n=1.", "evidence": "A monotone path from (1,n) to (m,1) has exactly m-1 E steps and n-1 S steps. It is uniquely specified by choosing which m-1 of its m+n-2 step positions carry E, giving binomial(m+n-2,m-1). If m=1 or n=1, there are no coins and exactly one configuration; the same formula equals 1.", "verifiable": true, "status": "unverified", "fact_id": null, "links": {"plan_id": "hole-path-bijection", "attempt_type": "direct", "status": "solved", "subgoal": "enumeration", "counterexample_search_for_stuck_subgoal": {"performed": false, "result": "not_needed"}, "used_results": [], "adapted_from": [], "key_stuck_points": []}, "glossary": {"E": "A unit east step, increasing the first coordinate i by 1.", "S": "A unit south step, decreasing the second coordinate j by 1."}}
{"id": "2df140b72469b453", "timestamp_utc": "2026-08-28T11:59:30.305455+00:00", "author": "xhigh", "kind": "proof_attempt", "claim": "Solved subgoal: prove the empty-path invariant and identify the exact effect of every legal move.", "evidence": "The initial empty cells are the m+n-1 cells of the path E^(m-1)S^(n-1). A legal move in the 2-by-2 block with northwest cell (i,j) changes the empty triple (i,j+1),(i+1,j+1),(i+1,j), an ES corner, into (i,j+1),(i,j),(i+1,j), an SE corner. Hence the empty set remains a monotone path by induction, including degenerate paths when m=1 or n=1.", "verifiable": true, "status": "unverified", "fact_id": null, "links": {"plan_id": "path-corner-swap", "attempt_type": "direct", "used_examples": [], "used_counterexamples": [], "counterexample_search_for_stuck_subgoal": {"performed": false, "summary": "No stuck or fragile subgoal remained after the quantified argument.", "result": "not_needed"}, "key_stuck_points": [], "used_results": [], "adapted_from": ["MAA 2023 Putnam Session B, Solution 1 path-corner invariant; all details rederived self-containedly"], "migration_failures": [], "branch_id": "complete-target-proof", "subgoal": "Empty-path invariant", "attempt_summary": "Coordinate audit of the local 2-by-2 change proves the invariant.", "status": "solved"}, "glossary": {"E": "The lattice-path step (i,j) to (i+1,j).", "S": "The lattice-path step (i,j) to (i,j-1)."}}
{"id": "8959c7361e51b63c", "timestamp_utc": "2026-08-28T11:59:30.358248+00:00", "author": "xhigh", "kind": "proof_attempt", "claim": "Solved subgoal: every monotone E/S path from (1,n) to (m,1) is the empty set of a reachable configuration.", "evidence": "For a target path word, repeatedly replace an adjacent SE by ES. Such a pair exists unless all E steps precede all S steps, and each swap decreases the number of pairs in which S precedes E, so the process reaches E^(m-1)S^(n-1). At an SE corner with path cells (i,j+1),(i,j),(i+1,j), the fourth cell (i+1,j+1) is off the monotone path and thus occupied. Sliding its coin northwest to (i,j) realizes the reverse relation between the two path-complement configurations. Reversing all these slides gives legal southeast moves, because after the northwest slide the coin at (i,j) has precisely the other three cells empty.", "verifiable": true, "status": "unverified", "fact_id": null, "links": {"plan_id": "path-corner-swap", "attempt_type": "direct", "used_examples": [], "used_counterexamples": [], "counterexample_search_for_stuck_subgoal": {"performed": false, "summary": "No stuck or fragile subgoal remained after the quantified argument.", "result": "not_needed"}, "key_stuck_points": [], "used_results": [], "adapted_from": ["MAA 2023 Putnam Session B, Solution 1 path-corner invariant; all details rederived self-containedly"], "migration_failures": [], "branch_id": "complete-target-proof", "subgoal": "Surjectivity onto all monotone paths", "attempt_summary": "Reverse adjacent swaps with inversion-number termination give a legal forward construction.", "status": "solved"}, "glossary": {"E": "The lattice-path step (i,j) to (i+1,j).", "S": "The lattice-path step (i,j) to (i,j-1)."}}
{"id": "7009f1cc19a74e6e", "timestamp_utc": "2026-08-28T11:59:30.407574+00:00", "author": "xhigh", "kind": "proof_attempt", "claim": "Solved subgoal: distinct monotone paths give distinct configurations, and their number is binomial(m+n-2,m-1), with boundary cases included.", "evidence": "A path-complement configuration determines its empty-cell set. Two different path words have a first differing step; if one takes E and the other S, the E-first path's next cell can never be visited by the S-first path because its second coordinate has already decreased and can never increase. Thus their empty sets, hence configurations, differ. There are exactly binomial(m+n-2,m-1) words containing m-1 E steps and n-1 S steps. If m=1 or n=1, there are no coins, only one path word and one configuration, while the same binomial coefficient equals 1.", "verifiable": true, "status": "unverified", "fact_id": null, "links": {"plan_id": "path-corner-swap", "attempt_type": "direct", "used_examples": [], "used_counterexamples": [], "counterexample_search_for_stuck_subgoal": {"performed": false, "summary": "No stuck or fragile subgoal remained after the quantified argument.", "result": "not_needed"}, "key_stuck_points": [], "used_results": [], "adapted_from": ["MAA 2023 Putnam Session B, Solution 1 path-corner invariant; all details rederived self-containedly"], "migration_failures": [], "branch_id": "complete-target-proof", "subgoal": "Injectivity, enumeration, and boundary cases", "attempt_summary": "First-divergence monotonicity proves injectivity; choosing E positions gives the binomial count.", "status": "solved"}, "glossary": {"E": "The lattice-path step (i,j) to (i+1,j).", "S": "The lattice-path step (i,j) to (i,j-1)."}}

